#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355380 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b7af875-8f86-5068-981f-28022f2d00fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355382} {:answer/id #uuid "7af771c3-4e6c-526b-aec4-2b21a13cea87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "770" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355383} {:answer/id #uuid "12561444-5c40-5e0e-a78a-6be8584ceecd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "890" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355384} {:answer/id #uuid "eb866571-56be-54d8-a406-9209672bc5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355385} {:answer/id #uuid "1c4dc78f-49c1-5da3-b01c-9aa3b6e02ce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355386}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb866571-56be-54d8-a406-9209672bc5b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "980" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355385} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d211b4e-ff2a-53ac-9910-c22000b388af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "28a6dc88-cc83-5e2f-ad4d-41299804d15f" :question/math-academy-id "q-116621" :question/problem "The following table shows the number of customers per minute, $c(t),$ that arrive at a shop counter over a period of $120$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $30$ | $80$ | $120$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $c(t)\\,(\\text{customers/minute})$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $12$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,30]\\cup [30,80]\\cup [80,120],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total number of customers served in two hours." :question/worked-solution "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=c(t)$ represents the total number of customers served in $2$ hours.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{c(0)+c(30)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{c(30)+c(80)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{c(80)+c(120)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{10+8}{2}\\cdot (30-0)+\\frac{8+6}{2}\\cdot (80-30)+\\frac{6+12}{2}\\cdot (120-80) \\\\  & =9\\cdot 30+7\\cdot 50+9\\cdot 40 \\\\  & =980\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total number of customers serve approximately is $980$ in $2$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "903cd6d7-18f2-5b53-a8e9-9aaef5589549" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule: Word Problem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea913ff8-58a5-5fda-9f60-639c6f082d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1750" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355389}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea913ff8-58a5-5fda-9f60-639c6f082d73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1750" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355389} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c78c5ae-39ac-51c4-8f3c-ea9c526a3b28" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355388}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5ea5af74-d9e6-5ec0-a5a9-05bf839b14a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-315743" :question/problem "The following table shows the inflow rate of water, $q(t),$ in $\\text{L/min},$ into a storage tank over a period of $90$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $20$ | $60$ | $90$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $q(t)\\,(\\text{L/min})$ | $15$ | $25$ | $20$ | $10$ |\n\nUse the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[0,20]\\cup [20,60]\\cup [60,90],\n$$\n\nto approximate the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nThe total volume is {{field-1}} liters." :question/worked-solution "In this context, we have $3$ trapezoids, and the area $A$ under the curve $y=q(t)$ represents the total volume of water that flowed into the tank in $90$ minutes.\n\nUsing the trapezoidal rule, we estimate the area as follows:\n\n$$\nA≈\\frac{q(0)+q(20)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{1}+\\frac{q(20)+q(60)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{2}+\\frac{q(60)+q(90)}{2}\\cdot Δ{t}_{3}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{15+25}{2}\\cdot (20-0)+\\frac{25+20}{2}\\cdot (60-20)+\\frac{20+10}{2}\\cdot (90-60) \\\\  & =20\\cdot 20+22.5\\cdot 40+15\\cdot 30 \\\\  & =400+900+450 \\\\  & =1750.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total volume of water that flowed into the tank is $1750$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2960d2d6-7354-5f2a-80cc-8d514465455f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Trapezoidal Rule Sum Gives an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d04e09ab-f907-5f79-9a7e-aa35930396e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090558} {:answer/id #uuid "d62da0be-bbdc-59b8-a24c-ed617ad388e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-5.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090559} {:answer/id #uuid "30458f49-b412-5360-a761-5945107747a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090560} {:answer/id #uuid "7907f925-ce0c-512c-bfd6-1e5e7ec65b4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090561} {:answer/id #uuid "79bb27e0-9480-5e27-a7e2-79f64fd54b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d04e09ab-f907-5f79-9a7e-aa35930396e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/945/Source/Images/q-49157-a-1.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090558} :answer-field/id #uuid "7de21e88-17de-5f10-8b6c-a61d486cdc08" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186090557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f5a7b0f-d085-5af5-bac3-ba61015f4796" :question/math-academy-id "q-49157" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an overestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-1$ and $x=3?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule overestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave up over the given interval.    \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave up on $[-1,3]$ is the one shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/e5663868b6caa013097b70233d9461227410f9dd6be9113620a67501b8466abf.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2960d2d6-7354-5f2a-80cc-8d514465455f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether the Trapezoidal Rule Sum Gives an Underestimate or Overestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb3ee0bd-3931-536b-8eff-273197f6312e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/8ba32de87efa6d2aaf854518f5d06f74699cb606ffa48a55eef5ffd4ec64322d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355392} {:answer/id #uuid "62f64aa0-6dfd-5a12-a5ae-2252e0ccc50d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/1d0f63c1fed5fae266f0f7689a16a277212a947628d1e2af00ba32382ff631b7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355393} {:answer/id #uuid "ccb37e5b-25af-5b09-8339-fefc1e603648" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/ff8301496a51f809d3aaa6e1a5336b39ce26171f2f1ab02f3d9a169bc0357963.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355394} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef8da82-287a-5cba-bac5-64c0732fe13a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/55ac4ca541d0cc5647fe9db98ba3c40f28558552682eb98a40c06458064aeec7.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355395} {:answer/id #uuid "20aee8bc-fac2-5d86-924d-259fcbf3ee15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/a8bcad7d4e90c6fdde99189026764d2b07a0709b8450252250c4fa133f5ceec0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20aee8bc-fac2-5d86-924d-259fcbf3ee15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/a8bcad7d4e90c6fdde99189026764d2b07a0709b8450252250c4fa133f5ceec0.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355396} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef8920a5-eae9-5138-b26a-ba101d6a76a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0f96be30-b6f7-514c-9651-7513613567d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-85673" :question/problem "For which of the following graphs would the trapezoidal rule give an underestimate of the area under the curve between $x=-1$ and $x=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The trapezoidal rule underestimates the area under the curve if the function is concave down over the given interval.     \n\nFrom the given graphs, the only curve that is concave down on $[-1,1]$ is the one shown below. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/a8bcad7d4e90c6fdde99189026764d2b07a0709b8450252250c4fa133f5ceec0.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186355397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bec73486-156f-5124-97b4-58f65ea8430d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Trapezoidal Rule With Irregular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93852cd4-58a9-58b3-bc31-4716f12863c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355399} {:answer/id #uuid "e6ab4d9e-d2cb-585d-bc14-d013c043fc97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{75}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355400} {:answer/id #uuid "b74dcd95-83bc-5359-826c-1fbbe98d74f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355401} {:answer/id #uuid "d5a03eb5-bab1-5ccb-8e8d-0960a17af8d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{35}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355402} {:answer/id #uuid "75df8197-27d6-507e-9dd8-604c1896963f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355403}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b74dcd95-83bc-5359-826c-1fbbe98d74f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{39}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355401} :answer-field/id #uuid "719956ff-3787-51e9-8a15-e4cd9eb2e858" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186355398}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9177065a-e07c-56ec-9cd6-608f8a33a326" :question/math-academy-id "q-85683" :question/problem "Using the trapezoidal rule with the partition\n\n$$\n[-1,2]=[-1,0]\\cup [0,2],\n$$\n\napproximate the area under the curve of the function $y=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=\\frac{10}{1+{x}^{2}}.$ Let's plot this function:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067661/assets/c4c9aa8e615747199462e0597f4cb7be82031efdb5b2f6585e3fb9c0b652ee78.png)\n\nWe've drawn some trapezoids to help calculate our trapezoidal rule sum. By calculating the area of each trapezoid, we obtain the following approximation for our area $A:$\n\n$$\nA≈\\overset{1\\text{st trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(-1)+f(0)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{1}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}+\\overset{2\\text{nd trapezoid}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\,\\frac{\\overset{\\text{bases}}{\\overset{⏞}{f(0)+f(2)}}}{2}\\,\\cdot \\,{{Δ{x}_{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\text{height}}\\,]}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the appropriate numbers into the above expression gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈\\frac{5+10}{2}\\cdot (0-(-1))+\\frac{10+2}{2}\\cdot (2-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 1+\\frac{12}{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}+12 \\\\  & =\\frac{39}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
